   "id": "y2-17",
   "name": "Further study 2: factors in the decline of African empires"
  }
 ],
 "note": {
  "status": "draft",
  "text": "The great African states declined for internal and external reasons.\nInternal factors:\n• weak or cruel rulers after strong ones;\n• quarrels and civil wars over succession;\n• empires too large to control, so provinces and vassal states revolted;\n• loss of resources: drought, overgrazing, exhausted soils and firewood (Great Zimbabwe).\nExternal factors:\n• invasions: the Almoravids attacked Ghana (1076); the Tuareg and Songhai attacked Mali; Morocco destroyed Songhai at Tondibi (1591);\n• changes in trade routes: when trade moved from the Sahara to the Atlantic coast and Europeans, the inland empires lost income;\n• the Atlantic slave trade took people and caused wars (Kongo);\n• European conquest later.\nUsually several factors worked together.",
  "checks": []
 },
 "worked_example": {
  "status": "draft",
  "vars": {
   "e": "Songhai"
  },
  "problem": "Give one internal and one external factor in the decline of {e}.",
  "steps": [
   "Internal: quarrels over succession weakened the rulers.",
   "External: the Moroccan army with firearms defeated Songhai at Tondibi in {1591}."
  ],
  "answer": "Succession quarrels (internal) and the Moroccan invasion (external).",
  "checks": []
 },
